<section class="section" id="method">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <span class="section-tag">How It's Built</span>
      <h2>Contributed by owners. Corrected by owners.</h2>
      <p>
        This isn't a factory record — it's owner submissions and club rosters, reviewed
        before they go live, with all the mess that implies.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cards3">
      <div class="note-card reveal">
        <div class="n mono">01</div>
        <h3>Data gets stale</h3>
        <p>
          Entries come from owners and clubs around the world and can lag behind reality
          — cars change hands, get parted out, or vanish long before anyone updates a
          roster. Spot an error and you can file a correction against the entry.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-card reveal">
        <div class="n mono">02</div>
        <h3>Model year is judgment</h3>
        <p>
          Where manufacturing date and first registration disagree, registration wins —
          dealers often re-badged unsold cars to the next model year, so the factory
          sequence is treated as the tiebreaker. Both dates are stored either way.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-card reveal">
        <div class="n mono">03</div>
        <h3>Modified cars still count</h3>
        <p>
          V6 and V8 swaps, the GT6-engined "Spit 6" conversion — all included, as long
          as the car still carries its original commission number. Lose the plate and
          the entry moves to the archive.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
